.TH DIPIXY 1 "2026-08-28" "dvbipitools" "dvbipitools Manual"
.SH NAME
dipixy \- serve DVB-IPI multicast streams over HTTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dipixy
[\fB\-l\fR \fIaddr\fR:\fIport\fR] [\fB\-i\fR \fIsource\fR ...] [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dipixy
is an amped up version of
.BR udpxy .
Not a fork, more like a tribute. On steroids. It can also act as a
bridge between DVB-IPI and the more modern DVB-I-ish world.
.PP
While
.B http://\fIaddress\fR:\fIport\fR/\fIcmd\fR/[\fIsrc_address\fR@]\fImgroup_address\fR<sep>\fImgroup_port\fR/
still works like at
.BR udpxy ,
a few things were added:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Protocols: IPv6, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, HTTPS
.IP \(bu 2
Ingress: MPTS demux, RIST, SRT, stdin
.IP \(bu 2
Egress: HLS (TS and fMP4), Low-Latency HLS, MPEG-DASH (regular and CMAF Low-Latency), audio raw demux
.IP \(bu 2
Operational: OpenMetrics
.IP \(bu 2
Convenience: a "URL Builder", PID filters, and playlists (M3U, XSPF,
CSV, TVA XML, SD&S client \- see
.BR dipiscan (1)
and
.BR dipisds (1))
.RE
.PP
What behaves differently:
.B rtp
vs
.B udp
as
.I cmd
is strict and not just about probe skipping.
.PP
What's lost, compared to
.BR udpxy :
udpxrec (
.BR dipirec (1)
can serve a similar purpose); it's not lightweight; no other
character than
.B :
separates \fImcast_group\fR:\fIport\fR; and its environment variables.
.SH INPUT
.B \-i
is repeatable and dipixy dispatches each occurrence by the form of its
argument:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-
\- stdin, served at
.BR /stdin/ <fmt>.
At most one.
.IP \(bu 2
.RI rist://@ host : port
\- RIST input, served at
.BR /rist/ <fmt>.
At most one.
.IP \(bu 2
.RI sds:// addr : port
\- live SD&S/DVBSTP discovery.
.IP \(bu 2
.RI http[s]:// url
\- single TS push URI.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI *. m3u\fR/\fB.m3u8
\- M3U playlist.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI *. xspf
\- XSPF playlist.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI *. csv
\- CSV playlist.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI *. xml
\- SD&S BroadcastDiscovery TVA XML.
.RE
.PP
M3U, XSPF, and CSV are not strictly limited to the output of
.BR dipiscan (1).
.PP
Every
.BR sds:// ,
playlist, and
.B http[s]://
source shares one index space, numbered in the order it was given on
the command line, so the first of these three kinds is list 1, the
2nd is list 2, and so on.
.PP
.BR rist://@ ,
.B \-
(stdin), or
.B http[s]://
each define just a single stream, not a playlist.
.PP
.BR \-n " \fIname\fR"
right after an
.B \-i
gives that input a name, usable in URLs instead of its
.BR /list/ n/
index or its bare
.BR /rist/ / /stdin/
route (see URL PATHS below). Rules for
.IR name :
no
.BR / ,
no leading
.BR . ,
not one of
.BR rtp ", " udp ", " srt ", " rist ", " stdin ", " list ", " metrics ", " ui ", " api ", " dlna ,
and unique across every named
.BR \-i .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-iface " \fIiface\fR"
Interface for multicast joins. Default: kernel default route.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-listen " \fIaddr\fR:\fIport\fR"
HTTP listen address:port. Default: all:9080.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-listen\-tls " \fIaddr\fR:\fIport\fR"
HTTPS listen address:port. Default: all:9443.
.TP
.B \-\-tls\-cert \fIpath\fR
Certificate file (PEM). Default: search default paths, see TLS below.
.TP
.B \-\-tls\-key \fIpath\fR
Private key file (PEM). Default: search default paths, see TLS below.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-workers " \fIspec\fR"
.BR \-1 / \-2 / \-3 :
that many times the cpu core count, or
.RI < N >:
an absolute thread count. Default: \-1.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-max\-clients " \fIn\fR"
Cap on concurrent streams. Default: 256.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-channels \fIn\fR
Cap on concurrent (source,filter,pmt,container) stores. Default: 32.
.TP
.B \-\-capture\-ring\-size \fIKiB\fR
Per\-source ingress ring buffer. Default: 4096.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-input " \fIsource\fR"
Add an input, repeatable, dispatched by form; see INPUT above. List
index = position among all
.B \-i
flags, so a
.B \-
or
.B rist://
slot leaves that number unused.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " \fIname\fR"
Names the
.B \-i
right before it; that name can then be used in URLs instead of
.BR /list/ n/
or
.BR /rist/ / /stdin .
Per-input; default: none (unnamed, addressable by index only).
.TP
.BI \-\-media\-type " radio\fR|\fBtv"
.B radio
or
.B tv
for the
.B \-i
right before it, drives DLNA
.BR upnp:class .
Per-input; default: tv.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Skip TLS verification on an
.B https://
input. Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-sds\-timeout \fIseconds\fR
.B sds://
discovery wait at startup/reload. Default: 3.
.TP
.B \-\-sds\-refresh\-interval \fIseconds\fR
.B sds://
re\-poll period. Default: 30.
.TP
.B \-\-segment\-size \fIseconds\fR
Target segment duration
.RB ( hls ", " hls\-fmp4 ", " llhls ", " dash ", " lldash ).
Default: 3.
.TP
.B \-\-segment\-count \fIn\fR
Playlist/manifest sliding-window size. Default: 4.
.TP
.B \-\-hls\-part\-size \fIseconds\fR
LL\-HLS target part duration, must be
.B <
.BR \-\-segment\-size .
Default: 0.35.
.TP
.B \-\-dash\-part\-size \fIseconds\fR
LL\-DASH target chunk duration, must be
.B <
.BR \-\-segment\-size .
Default: 0.333.
.TP
.B \-\-dash\-utc\-url \fIurl\fR
LL\-DASH MPD UTCTiming source (max 256 chars). Default:
.IR http://time.akamai.com/?iso&ms .
.TP
.B \-\-hls\-seg\-pool \fIn\fR
Segment buffer freelist cap per size class. Raise on a memory\-rich
edge server to cut malloc under many concurrent HLS streams. Default: 8.
.TP
.B \-\-metrics \fIpath\fR
Unix datagram socket for metrics. Default:
.IR /run/dvbipitools/metrics.sock .
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-id \fIname\fR
Stable instance id. Default: none (metrics disabled unless set).
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-interval \fIs\fR
Snapshot interval. Default: 5.
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-http
Also serve
.B /metrics
ourselves. Default: off.
.TP
.BI "\-f, \-\-format " list
Comma-separated route whitelist, from
.BR ts ", " spts ", " rawaudio ", " hls ", " llhls ", " dash ", " lldash
(
.B hls
covers both
.B hls
and
.B hls\-fmp4
routes). Formats not listed are disabled. Default: all enabled.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-url\-rtp
Deactivate
.BR /rtp/ .
Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-url\-udp
Deactivate
.BR /udp/ .
Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-url\-srt
Deactivate
.BR /srt/ .
Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pid\-filters
Deactivate
.BR ?filter= .
Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-http2
disable HTTP/2, default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-http3
disable HTTP/3, default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fcc
Ignore SDS fcc. Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ret
Ignore SDS ret. Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-status
Deactivate
.BR /ui/status.js .
Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-status\-tpl \fIpath\fR
Use this file instead of the built-in status page. Default: built-in.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth " user\fR:\fBpass"
HTTP Basic Auth for
.BR / ", " /ui/status.js ", and " /ui/ws/ .
Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-cors\-origin \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated allowlist against
.B Origin
for
.BR hls / hls\-fmp4 / llhls / dash / lldash
responses. Default:
.BR * .
.TP
.B \-\-ssdp\-ttl \fIn\fR
Multicast TTL for SSDP packets. Default: 3.
.TP
.B \-\-ssdp\-iface \fIiface\fR
Interface for SSDP announce/reply. Default: kernel default route.
.TP
.B \-\-ssdp\-interval \fIseconds\fR
SSDP NOTIFY re\-announce period. Default: 60.
.TP
.B \-\-ssdp\-max\-age \fIseconds\fR
Advertised
.B CACHE\-CONTROL
max\-age, must be
.B >=
2x
.BR \-\-ssdp\-interval .
Default: 1800.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-dlna
Serve SSDP and a UPnP MediaServer (DLNA), see DLNA below. Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-dlna\-host \fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]
Host[:port] advertised in SSDP/DIDL. Default:
.BR \-\-listen ,
if not
.BR all .
.TP
.B \-\-dlna\-name \fIname\fR
DLNA friendlyName. Default:
.RI dipixy\ ( dlna\-host ).
.TP
.B \-\-dlna\-keep\-multicast
.BR rtp / udp
items point straight at their mgroup instead of
.BR /spts ,
see DLNA below. Default: off.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemonize
Fork to background after startup, detach from terminal. Default: off.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Per-connection diagnostics on stderr. Default: off.
.TP
.B \-\-color \fIwhen\fR
.BR auto | always | never .
Default: auto.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH URL PATHS
There is a shortcut: start
.B dipixy
with all your inputs and open
.BR http://127.0.0.1:9080/ .
The "URL Builder" lets you craft them easily.
.PP
The included web interface is basic on purpose.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
It's compiled-in.
.IP \(bu 2
Any web-based video player for HLS/LL-HLS/DASH would still hit some
codec walls (AC3, E-AC3).
.IP \(bu 2
By staying a static website that's easy to read, it also functions as
a kind of documentation:
.B /ui/status.js
is a JSON object with all "startup" info,
.B /ui/ws/
is the WebSocket for all dynamic information.
.IP \(bu 2
Using
.BR \-\-status\-tpl ,
you are invited to roll your own replacement; SIGHUP reloads it at
runtime, easing your development.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI \-\-auth\ user : pass
puts
.BR / ", " /ui/status.js " and " /ui/ws/
behind HTTP Basic Auth (checked on HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3
alike). Every other route stays open.
.RE
.PP
Anyway, here are the details. These routes address a source directly
(\fIfmt\fR is one of \fBts\fR, \fBspts\fR, \fBrawaudio\fR, \fBhls\fR,
\fBhls\-fmp4\fR, \fBllhls\fR, \fBdash\fR, \fBlldash\fR):
.PP
.RS
/rtp/\fIaddr\fR:\fIport\fR/\fIfmt\fR    (RTP source)
.br
/udp/\fIaddr\fR:\fIport\fR/\fIfmt\fR    (plain MPEG-TS over UDP source)
.br
/srt/\fIaddr\fR:\fIport\fR/\fIfmt\fR    (SRT source, caller mode, dedup'd by addr:port)
.RE
.PP
These two are bare, with no address, and at most one of each can ever
exist:
.PP
.RS
/rist/\fIfmt\fR    (the \fB\-i rist://@\fR\fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR source)
.br
/stdin/\fIfmt\fR   (the \fB\-i \-\fR source)
.RE
.PP
These reach a channel list, indexed in
.B \-i
definition order:
.PP
.RS
/list/\fIn\fR/item/\fIi\fR/\fIfmt\fR
.br
/list/\fIn\fR/name/\fIchannel\fR/\fIfmt\fR
.RE
.PP
A named
.B \-i
(via
.BR \-n ,
see OPTIONS) is reachable the same way,
.I name
standing in for the whole bare or
.BR /list/ n/
piece \- both forms keep working side by side:
.PP
.RS
/\fIname\fR/\fIfmt\fR    (\fB\-i \-\fR or \fB\-i rist://@\fR\fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR)
.br
/\fIname\fR/item/\fIi\fR/\fIfmt\fR
.br
/\fIname\fR/name/\fIchannel\fR/\fIfmt\fR
.RE
.PP
.I fmt
can be omitted (with or without a trailing slash); it then defaults to
.BR ts .
.PP
Any path above accepts
.BI ?filter= pids
to drop PIDs from the output (comma-separated decimal or 0x-hex, e.g.
.BR ?filter=101,0x20 ).
Disable this feature with
.BR \-\-no\-pid\-filters .
.PP
On a multi-program transport stream (MPTS),
.B ts
always passes every program through unchanged.
.BR spts / hls / hls\-fmp4 / llhls / dash / lldash
demux a single program and default to the first PMT that resolves.
You can override this by selecting a specific PMT:
.BI ?pmt= pid
(decimal or 0x-hex).
.B spts
is a continuous raw TS push like
.BR ts ,
just guaranteed single-program: only the locked program's
PAT/PMT/PCR/ES PIDs go out.
.PP
.B rawaudio
locks the same way
.RB ( ?pmt= ,
default first PMT that resolves), then picks that program's
lowest-numbered audio ES that is not dropped by
.B ?filter=
and forwards its PES payload as-is: raw data.
.SH CODECS
.B ts / spts
and plain
.B hls
(TS segments) are a straight TS remux: any video/audio codec passes
through untouched. Segmenting still needs to locate keyframes,
understood for MPEG-2 Video, H.264/AVC and HEVC/H.265; other video
codecs won't cut cleanly on an IDR/I-frame.
.PP
.BR hls\-fmp4 ", " llhls ", " dash " and " lldash
build actual ISOBMFF (fMP4) sample entries, so their codec support is
narrower:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
video: H.264/AVC, HEVC/H.265. MPEG-2 Video has no fMP4 sample entry
and won't produce output.
.IP \(bu 2
audio: AAC (ADTS or LATM), AC-3, Enhanced AC-3 (E-AC-3), MPEG-1 Layer
II (MP2).
.RE
.PP
.B rawaudio
just forwards a program's lowest-numbered audio ES's PES payload
as-is, codec-agnostic.
.SH DLNA
.B dipixy
comes with an integrated DLNA server, disabled by default.
.PP
If enabled
.RB ( \-\-enable\-dlna ),
dipixy announces itself as a UPnP MediaServer and serves a browsable
ContentDirectory tree. You can override the announced hostname using
.BR \-\-dlna\-host .
If your network needs extra hops,
.B \-\-ssdp\-ttl
lets you customize its reach.
.PP
Assign a
.BR \-n " \fIname\fR"
to your inputs if you want nicer list names in your UPnP tree.
.PP
If an input was marked as
.BR \-\-media\-type\ radio ,
the stream(s) affected get flagged as
.B upnp:class = object.item.audioItem.audioBroadcast
and served as
.BR /rawaudio .
.B tv
is always
.BR /spts .
.PP
.B \-\-dlna\-name
lets you set a nicer name to appear on your TV (like "MyProvider TV").
.PP
M3U's
.B tvg\-logo
and XSPF's
.BR <image> ,
as long as they point to a webserver, will get used as channel logos
/ picons if present.
.PP
.B \-\-dlna\-keep\-multicast
skips the http proxy for items whose source is a multicast
.BR rtp:// " or " udp://
group, whether from
.B sds://
discovery or a playlist entry: their DIDL
.B res
points the renderer straight at the mgroup via
.RB protocolInfo=\(dq dvb\-igmp:*:33:* \(dq
(IPv4) or
.B dvb\-mld:*:33:*
(IPv6), per ETSI TS 102 905.
.BR srt:// /http(s)
playlist entries and stdin/rist/http(s)
.B \-i
sources are unicast and always keep going through
.BR /spts .
.SH TLS
If
.BR \-\-tls\-cert / \-\-tls\-key
aren't given, dipixy looks for
.BR server.crt / server.key
in the current working directory, in
.BR /etc/dvbipitools/ ,
and in
.BR /etc/dvbipitools/dipixy/ .
If none are found,
.B \-\-listen\-tls
is simply left unbound, and that is not an error. SIGUSR1 reloads the
certificate without a restart.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
dipixy always speaks HTTP/1.1. HTTP/2
.RI ( libnghttp2 )
and HTTP/3
.RI ( libngtcp2 " + " libngtcp2_crypto_ossl " + " libnghttp3 )
are each auto-detected at build time, and TLS itself needs OpenSSL.
Missing any of these disables that feature rather than failing the
build.
.BR \-\-no\-http2 " and " \-\-no\-http3
turn either off at runtime regardless of what the binary was built
with, and skip that protocol's connection buffers entirely.
.PP
RIST and SRT input need
.IR librist / libsrt
the same as every other tool here.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B ^C ", " SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Stop.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read every
.BR sds:// /playlist
source and swap in the result; a source that fails to reload keeps
serving its last-known-good list rather than going empty.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reload the TLS certificate.
.SH BUGS
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Opening any form of HLS or DASH streams on a "cold" ingress stream
means that there are no pre-produced segments available. This means
that playback cannot start immediately.
.IP \(bu 2
RIST and SRT don't support bonding in this tool. You can use
.BR dipirist (1)
or
.BR dipisrt (1)
with
.B \-i \-
as a work-around.
.IP \(bu 2
The multicast client does not support FEC.
.IP \(bu 2
Radio. DVB-IPI installations wildly differ on how they implemented
this. Some deliver an SPTS with one Audio PES. Others go full OTT,
deliver raw streams or pack them into an MPTS until it's the size of
their smallest TV profile. Similar for client support. VLC or Kodi
will happily play a DLNA radio playlist, Samsung or LG most probably
won't \- or maybe they do.
.IP \(bu 2
No transcoding here. While bursts won't be a problem, your DVB-IPI
provider dislikes them as much as you do, heavy use of B-frames or a
long GoP will collide with short segment sizes in HLS or DASH.
.IP \(bu 2
M3U and XSPF input playlists can contain
.BR rtp:// ", " udp:// ", " srt://
or HTTP(S) TS Push, not RIST, HLS or DASH.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dipixy \-l 0.0.0.0:9080
Direct routes, no channel list needed:
.RS
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/udp/239.1.1.1:5000/ts
(TS push)
.br
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/rtp/239.1.1.1:5000/dash
(MPEG DASH)
.br
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/srt/1.2.3.4:9000/hls
(HLS, TS segments)
.br
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/srt/1.2.3.4:9000/hls\-fmp4
(HLS, fMP4 segments)
.br
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/srt/1.2.3.4:9000/llhls
(Low-Latency HLS)
.RE
.TP
.B dipixy \-i sds://239.19.75.1:3937
Live SD&S discovery as list 1.
.TP
.B dipixy \-l 0.0.0.0:9080 \-i channels.m3u
A static M3U channel list, custom port.
.TP
.B dipixy \-L [::]:9443 \-\-tls\-cert server.crt \-\-tls\-key server.key \-i tv.xspf \-i radio.xspf
HTTPS on IPv6, 2 XSPF lists.
.TP
.B ffmpeg \-re \-i source.mkv \-c copy \-f mpegts \- | dipixy \-i \- \-l 0.0.0.0:9080
Stdin, fed by ffmpeg; then
.BR "curl http://localhost:9080/stdin/hls\-fmp4" .
.TP
.B dipixy \-i rist://@0.0.0.0:9000 \-i https://origin.example/live.ts
A RIST listener plus a pulled HTTP(S) source, indexed as list 1:
.RS
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/rist/dash
.br
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/list/1/item/1/llhls
.RE
.TP
.B "vlc \(dqhttp://localhost:9080/udp/239.1.1.1:5000/llhls?filter=0x100,0x101&pmt=0x1000\(dq"
LL-HLS, PID-filtered, one program picked out of an MPTS (by the PID
of its PMT).
.TP
.B "vlc \(dqhttp://localhost:9080/udp/239.1.1.1:5000/spts?pmt=0x1000\(dq"
Guaranteed single-program TS push out of an MPTS, same PMT selection
as above.
.TP
.B dipixy \-l 0.0.0.0:9080 \-\-metrics\-id xy1
Metrics alongside
.BR dipimetrics (1).
.TP
.B dipixy \-i tv.xspf \-n tv \-i rist://@0.0.0.0:9000 \-n backup
Named inputs, addressed by name instead of index:
.RS
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/tv/item/1/hls
.br
.B vlc http://localhost:9080/backup/ts
.RE
.TP
.B dipixy \-l 0.0.0.0:9080 \-i channels.m3u \-\-enable\-dlna \-\-dlna\-host 192.0.2.10:9080
DLNA MediaServer, browsable by any UPnP renderer on the LAN.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B nonzero
Argument error, or listen-socket setup failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dipiscan (1),
.BR dipisds (1),
.BR dipirist (1),
